<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish hussars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as winged hussars, husaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish hussars, or husaria, were heavily armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, active from the sixteenth to the eighteenth century. They are known for the wooden frames bearing feathers, the wings, fixed to their armour. Their disciplined shock charges were decisive in many battles, including the relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-hussars.jpg" alt="Polish hussars" width="269" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Produced by Warszawa Salon Kulikowskiego · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Straz_hetmanska.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
